   167	---
   168	name: code-quality
   169	description: General Correctness rules, Rust patterns, comments, avoiding over-engineering. When writing code always take these into account
   170	---
   171	# Code Quality Guide
   172	
   173	## Core Principle
   174	
   175	Production database. Correctness paramount. Crash > corrupt.
   176	
   177	## Correctness Rules
   178	
   179	1. **No workarounds or quick hacks.** Handle all errors, check invariants
   180	2. **Assert often.** Never silently fail or swallow edge cases
   181	3. **Crash on invalid state** if it risks data integrity. Don't continue in undefined state
   182	4. **Consider edge cases.** On long enough timeline, all possible bugs will happen
   183	
   184	## Rust Patterns
   185	
   186	- Make illegal states unrepresentable
   187	- Exhaustive pattern matching
   188	- Prefer enums over strings/sentinels
   189	- Minimize heap allocations
   190	- Write CPU-friendly code (microsecond = long time)
   191	
   192	## If-Statements
   193	
   194	Wrong:
   195	```rust
   196	if condition {
   197	    // happy path
   198	} else {
   199	    // "shouldn't happen" - silently ignored
   200	}
   201	```
   202	
   203	Right:
   204	```rust
   205	// If only one branch should ever be hit:
   206	assert!(condition, "invariant violated: ...");
   207	// OR
   208	return Err(LimboError::InternalError("unexpected state".into()));
   209	// OR
   210	unreachable!("impossible state: ...");
   211	```
   212	
   213	Use if-statements only when both branches are expected paths.
   214	
   215	## Comments
   216	
   217	**Do:**
   218	- Document WHY, not what
   219	- Document functions, structs, enums, variants
   220	- Focus on why something is necessary
   221	
   222	**Don't:**
   223	- Comments that repeat code
   224	- References to AI conversations ("This test should trigger the bug")
   225	- Temporal markers ("added", "existing code", "Phase 1")
   226	
   227	## Avoid Over-Engineering
   228	
   229	- Only changes directly requested or clearly necessary
   230	- Don't add features beyond what's asked
   231	- Don't add docstrings/comments to unchanged code
   232	- Don't add error handling for impossible scenarios
   233	- Don't create abstractions for one-time operations
   234	- Three similar lines > premature abstraction
   235	
   236	## Semantic Ownership
   237	
   238	When an LLM or Skill is the declared owner of an open-ended semantic judgment:
   239	
   240	- Put project-specific rubrics, examples, labels, evidence standards, and model instructions in the owning project Skill first. Do not place domain rules in a global Skill merely to avoid writing them in production code; global Skills should retain only rules that are valid across projects.
   241	- Do not implement that judgment in production code with regexes, keyword lists, substring checks, text length, punctuation, color alone, or hand-tuned scores.
   242	- Do not patch a false positive by adding another phrase exception. Remove the misplaced classifier and keep the value `null` or `unknown` until the semantic owner supplies it.
   243	- Keep source facts and semantic annotations in separate provenance layers. Deterministic code may parse exact syntax, preserve evidence, validate schemas and references, and recompute metrics from accepted annotations.
   244	- A heuristic may only create an explicitly labeled review candidate that cannot enter production metrics or conclusions before confirmation.
   245	- Test that semantic outputs cannot overwrite source text, identity, coordinates, time, order, or deduplication facts.
   246	
   247	## Ensure understanding of IO model
   248	
   249	- [Async IO model](../async-io-model/SKILL.md)
   250	
   251	## Cleanup
   252	
   253	- Delete unused code completely
   254	- No backwards-compat hacks (renamed `_vars`, re-exports, `// removed` comments)
